public class ParallelS3Downloader {
    // Speed up downloads by pulling 5 parts of the file simultaneously from S3
    public void downloadInParallel(String objectKey, int totalSize) {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
        int chunkSize = totalSize / 5;

        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            final int start = i * chunkSize;
            final int end = (i == 4) ? totalSize : (start + chunkSize - 1);
            pool.submit(() -> {
                GetObjectRequest req = new GetObjectRequest("bucket", objectKey)
                        .withRange(start, end);
                s3Client.getObject(req);
            });
        }
    }
}

try-catch fallback mechanics:
1. ChecksumMismatchException: Upon downloading a chunk, the client hashes it. If the hash doesn't match the Metadata Database, silent bit-rot occurred on the hard drive. The S3 Gateway instantly discards the corrupted block, pulls a parity block from another rack, reconstructs the data in memory, returns it to the user, and triggers an asynchronous self-healing job on the degraded disk.
2. Total Node Failure: S3 doesn't 'repair a disk'. When a 10TB disk dies, S3 doesn't write 10TB back to an empty disk on the same box (which takes days). It sprays the 10TB of lost parity pieces across 10,000 different disks in the cluster, completing the 'array rebuild' in minutes to minimize the vulnerability window.
